From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:56:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] SP: a gamepad drives the menus, cut scenes and level
 briefings

The menus took a gamepad's buttons as keys of their own and did nothing
with them, and the game ate them where it waits for Enter, Space, Escape
or a click: a gamepad alone could not leave the main menu.

- Menus: the D-pad and the left stick work as the arrow keys, A as Enter.
  In a menu the left stick gives its four directions as keys (pushed past
  half way, let go below a third) whatever it is bound to; that binding
  sees it centred meanwhile. The right stick moves the mouse pointer: the
  menus are made for a mouse. Not while a binding waits for its key (the
  button itself is bound).
- One thing is chosen, the item under the pointer: keys that choose
  another item take the pointer onto it, and the pointer over an item of
  another menu (the tabs above a panel of the options) gives that menu
  the keys. Before, the pointer showed one item and Enter chose another,
  and the tabs could not be reached without a mouse.
- B goes back to the menu the focused one was opened from while that one
  is up (from a panel to its tabs, from a page of the in-game menu to
  it); otherwise it is Escape. Menus opened from one that Escape closes
  close with it (their onClose runs: the controls are saved).
  A page that a click outside closes (outOfBoundsClick: the pages of the
  in-game menu) closes when the keys leave it, as the click would: the
  next page opened over it before.
- Bindings: while one waits for its key, the keys go to it only. Escape
  (B) cancels it; it used to close every menu of the game in play and
  leave the binding waiting, to take whatever key came next. The sticks
  and Start are not bound from the menu.
- A level's briefing (pregame), which only a click on a button shown
  under the mouse ended: Enter and A start the level too.
- Cut scenes and cinematics: A, B and Start skip them as Enter, Space and
  Escape do.
